    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new field is wired in correctly and additively (schema across all four DB dialects, model getter/setter, OpenAPI, and a mapper mapping alongside the other derived fields), and the Javadoc matches the spec. But the age-band logic is not in the unit this file's own convention puts it in.\n\n**Business logic belongs in a model class, not inline in the mapper.** This mapper has an established, consistent idiom for derived fields:\n- Substantive derivation logic is delegated to a model/domain class \u2014 `toCheckDigit` \u2192 `LuhnCheckDigit.of(...)`, `toIdentityKey` \u2192 `OwnerIdentity.of(...)`, `toLocality` \u2192 `CityRegions.identityRegion(...)`.\n- Only trivial formatting stays inline as a default method \u2014 `toDisplayName`, `toInitials`, the one-line `toContactPreference`.\n\n`toAgeBand` is squarely in the first category: it does date arithmetic (`Period.between`) plus a multi-threshold classification (18/65) into named bands. That is exactly the shape of `CityRegions.identityRegion`, which maps a value into named ranges and lives in a model class. Leaving this classification table inline makes the mapper a catch-all for a rule that maintainers of this codebase would expect to find next to `LuhnCheckDigit`/`CityRegions`. Extract it to a model class (e.g. `AgeBand.of(birthDate, registrationDate)`) and have `toAgeBand` delegate, matching the neighbours.\n\n**Minor: fully-qualified type names break the file's style.** `java.time.LocalDate` / `java.time.Period` are written inline while the rest of the file (and `Owner`) uses top-of-file imports. Import `LocalDate`/`Period` and use the simple names for consistency. (This largely disappears if the logic moves to a model class as above.)",
